.TH IPCALC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ipcalc \- calculate IPv4 network settings from an address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ipcalc
.RB [ \-bnmphs ]
.I address\c
.RB [ /\c
.IR prefix ]
.RI [ netmask ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipcalc
works out properties of an IPv4 network from an address and its netmask.
It prints the ones selected by its options, one per line, or looks up
the host name of the address.
.PP
The first operand is the
.IR address ,
in dotted decimal, such as
.BR 192.168.1.5 .
The netmask that divides it into network and host bits is given in one
of two ways, but not both: a slash and a prefix length attached to the
address, as in
.BR 192.168.1.5/26 ,
or a second operand in dotted decimal, as in
.BR 255.255.255.192 .
With neither, the netmask is the default for the class of the address:
.B 255.0.0.0
when its first bit is 0, which is a first octet of 0 to 127;
.B 255.255.0.0
when its first two bits are 10, which is 128 to 191;
and
.B 255.255.255.0
otherwise, which is 192 to 255, including the multicast and reserved
ranges.
.PP
At least one of the options
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-h
is required.
.SS Output format
Each item selected by an option is a line
.IB name = value
on standard output.
The lines appear in the order below whatever the order of the options,
and the output suits the shell's
.B eval
for setting variables.
.TP
.BI NETMASK= address
The netmask, in dotted decimal, from
.BR \-m .
.TP
.BI BROADCAST= address
The broadcast address, from
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI NETWORK= address
The network address, from
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BI PREFIX= length
The prefix length, in decimal, from
.BR \-p .
.TP
.BI HOSTNAME= name
The host name, in lowercase, from
.BR \-h .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-bn ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix.
The
.I netmask
operand is accepted only when one of
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-n ,
or
.B \-p
is given, and is otherwise a usage error.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-broadcast
Print the broadcast address: the address with every bit outside the
netmask set to 1.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-network
Print the network address: the address with every bit outside the
netmask set to 0.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-netmask
Print the netmask in effect: the one given by the prefix or the
.I netmask
operand, or else the default for the class of the address.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix
Print the prefix length: the number of 1 bits in the netmask.
A netmask whose 1 bits are not contiguous is counted all the same, so
.B 255.0.255.0
gives 16.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-hostname
Print the host name of the address, as the Windows name resolver reports
it, converted to lowercase.
Only the first name is printed.
The prefix and the netmask play no part.
If the resolver finds no name, this is an error.
This option is not a request for help; see
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
Do not print error messages.
The exit status is unchanged.
Wrong arguments are still reported, with a usage message.
Give at least one other option with it.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, options must come before the operands.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B ipcalc
starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong arguments, or a failure.
.PP
Wrong arguments are an unknown or ambiguous option, no option from
.BR \-bnmph ,
a
.I netmask
operand where it is not accepted, or no operand or more than two.
They are reported with a usage message on standard error.
.PP
A failure is an
.I address
or
.I netmask
that cannot be read, a prefix that is not a number from 0 to 32, both a
prefix and a
.IR netmask ,
or no host name for the address.
It is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ipcalc: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
unless
.B \-s
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the network and broadcast addresses of a host on a network whose
prefix length is 26:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-bn 192.168.1.5/26
BROADCAST=192.168.1.63
NETWORK=192.168.1.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Do the same with a dotted netmask, and also show the prefix length:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-bnp 192.168.1.5 255.255.255.192
BROADCAST=192.168.1.63
NETWORK=192.168.1.0
PREFIX=26
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert a prefix length to a netmask:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-m 10.0.0.0/20
NETMASK=255.255.240.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the default netmask of a class B address:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-m 172.16.5.9
NETMASK=255.255.0.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set shell variables from the results:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ eval "$(ipcalc \-bn 172.16.5.9/12)"
$ echo $NETWORK $BROADCAST
172.16.0.0 172.31.255.255
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether an address is valid, with no output from
.B ipcalc
itself:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-sn 10.0.0.256 >/dev/null || echo invalid
invalid
.EE
.RE
.PP
Look up the name of an address:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ipcalc \-h 192.168.1.1
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Addresses and netmasks are read by the Windows
.B inet_addr
function.
Besides four decimal parts, it accepts fewer parts, where the last part
fills all the remaining bytes, so
.B 10.1
is
.B 10.0.0.1
and
.B 10
is
.BR 0.0.0.10 .
Each part may be hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix or octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
so
.B 010.010.010.010
is
.BR 8.8.8.8 .
Everything from the first white-space character on is ignored, except
that an operand of a single space reads as
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
Host names and IPv6 addresses are rejected, since only IPv4 is handled.
.PP
.B 255.255.255.255
is rejected, as an address and as a netmask, because
.B inet_addr
returns that value to report an error.
Write the prefix length instead, as in
.BR 10.0.0.1/32 .
.PP
The prefix is decimal digits only, with no sign, space, or other
characters, and leading zeros do not make it octal.
A slash with nothing after it is ignored.
A netmask operand is not checked for contiguous 1 bits.
.PP
The default netmask is chosen after the address is read, so an address
written with fewer than four parts, such as
.BR 200 ,
is
.B 0.0.0.200
and gets
.BR 255.0.0.0 .
.PP
Error messages name the address without the slash and prefix that may
follow it.
.PP
The host name is looked up with the Windows
.B gethostbyaddr
function, so it uses whatever the system resolver is configured to use,
including the hosts file.
Its failure message ends in
.BR "Unknown error" ,
because the Winsock error code is passed to the C library's message
table, which does not know it.
.PP
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
A write error on standard output is not detected, so the exit status is
still 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1)
